      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/tucanoichthys_tucano_en-3/",
      "html": "<p>A tiny, very nice and extremely rare dwarf tetra. It attains a maximum length of around 2 cm and is an ideal fish for nano aquaria.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/tucanoichthys_tucano_en-3/",
      "html": "<p>Tucanoichthys lives endemic in the upper Rio Negro basin, to be more precise in the Rio Uaupes.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "waterConditions",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/tucanoichthys_tucano_en-3/",
      "html": "<p>Fishes from that region should be kept in very soft, strongly acidic water (pH 5). The water should be coloured brownish with humic acids.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "dimorphism",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/tucanoichthys_tucano_en-3/",
      "html": "<p>Males have red in the caudal and anal fin, which is missing in females.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/tucanoichthys_tucano_en-3/",
      "html": "<p>In dark aquaria the pastel, but shining colours of the fish are presented best.</p>"
    }
